\b SchemaIQ Project Handoff - Frontend ER Diagram Focus\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Project Summary\b0\par
SchemaIQ is an AI-powered database intelligence platform with two backend areas and one React frontend.\par
\par
Main project goals:\par
- Query a database using AI and natural language.\par
- Inspect schema and relationships.\par
- Profile data quality.\par
- Generate and display ER diagrams.\par
\par
Your responsibility is mainly frontend-oriented:\par
- consume schema data from backend,\par
- convert schema JSON into graph data,\par
- render a custom ER diagram in React,\par
- improve readability, layout, and interactivity,\par
- avoid making risky backend changes that may conflict with teammates.\par
\par
\b 2. High-Level Architecture\b0\par
There are two important backend areas in this repo:\par
\par
1. Main backend at backend/\par
- Handles QueryBot, metrics, and app-level APIs.\par
- Main file: backend/main.py\par
- Runs on port 8000 in the README examples.\par
\par
2. Schema Intelligence Engine at backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/\par
- This is the service most relevant to your work.\par
- It exposes routes for schema extraction, profiling, preview, upload, and DB connection.\par
- Main file: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/main.py\par
- Runs on port 8001 in the README examples.\par
\par
3. Frontend at frontend/\par
- React + Vite app.\par
- Existing ER diagram UI currently uses mock data.\par
- Your main work should happen here.\par
\par
\b 3. What You Need for Your Part\b0\par
For the custom ER diagram task, you do not need to build schema extraction logic yourself.\par
You should mainly consume backend data from the Schema Intelligence Engine and use it in the frontend.\par
\par
Recommended responsibility split:\par
- Backend team / existing engine: provides schema JSON.\par
- You: fetch schema JSON, convert it to nodes and edges, and render the ER diagram.\par
\par
\b 4. Most Important Backend Folder for You\b0\par
Folder: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/\par
\par
Purpose:\par
- inspect a DB or uploaded files,\par
- extract tables, columns, keys, and relationships,\par
- return structured schema JSON,\par
- optionally infer missing relationships,\par
- provide preview and profiling data.\par
\par
\b 5. Routes You Should Know\b0\par
These routes are defined in backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/routes/.\par
\par
\b 5.1 POST /connect\b0\par
File: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/routes/connection.py\par
\par
Purpose:\par
- connect the schema engine to a live database URL.\par
\par
Request body:\par
\f1
{\pard
{\pntext\f1 1.\tab}{"db_url": "mysql+pymysql://user:pass@host:3306/dbname"}\par
}
\f0
\par
Response:\par
- {"status": "connected"}\par
\par
Use this if your team wants to inspect a real database connection instead of uploaded files.\par
\par
\b 5.2 POST /upload\b0\par
File: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/routes/upload.py\par
\par
Purpose:\par
- upload CSV or SQL files,\par
- convert them into SQLite,\par
- set that SQLite database as the active source.\par
\par
Supported inputs:\par
- .csv\par
- .sql\par
\par
Important behavior:\par
- CSV files go into a shared SQLite file: uploads/uploaded_data.sqlite\par
- SQL files create their own SQLite file.\par
\par
Response fields:\par
- status\par
- path\par
- db_url\par
- sqlite_path\par
\par
Use this if the frontend flow lets the user upload schema source files before viewing the ER diagram.\par
\par
\b 5.3 GET /schema\b0\par
File: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/routes/schema.py\par
\par
This is the most important route for your ER diagram work.\par
\par
Purpose:\par
- return canonical schema JSON,\par
- include tables, columns, primary keys, foreign keys, and relationships.\par
\par
Query params:\par
- db_url (optional)\par
- schema (optional)\par
- infer (optional boolean)\par
\par
Recommended first call:\par
\f1
GET /schema?infer=true\par
\f0
\par
Why use infer=true:\par
- if a dataset has no explicit foreign keys, the backend can infer likely relationships,\par
- which is helpful for ER diagram generation.\par
\par
Expected response shape:\par
\f1
{\pard
{\pntext\f1 1.\tab}{\par
  "tables": [\par
    {\par
      "name": "orders",\par
      "columns": [\par
        {"name": "order_id", "type": "string"},\par
        {"name": "customer_id", "type": "string"}\par
      ],\par
      "primary_key": ["order_id"],\par
      "foreign_keys": [\par
        {\par
          "column": "customer_id",\par
          "references": {"table": "customers", "column": "customer_id"}\par
        }\par
      ]\par
    }\par
  ],\par
  "relationships": [\par
    {"from_table": "orders", "to_table": "customers", "type": "many-to-one"}\par
  ]\par
}\par
}
\f0
\par
This is the exact backend output you should use to build your nodes and edges.\par
\par
\b 5.4 GET /profile\b0\par
File: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/routes/profile.py\par
\par
Purpose:\par
- return row counts, null percentages, distinct counts, and FK orphan checks.\par
\par
Why it may help you later:\par
- row counts can be shown in node tooltips,\par
- data quality information can enrich the schema explorer,\par
- but this route is not required for your first ER diagram MVP.\par
\par
\b 5.5 GET /preview\b0\par
File: backend/Schema Intelligence Engine/routes/preview.py\par
\par
Purpose:\par
- return limited sample rows for one table.\par
\par
Example:\par
\f1
GET /preview?table=orders&limit=10\par
\f0
\par
Why it may help:\par
- useful for debugging,\par
- useful for hover drawers or schema detail views,\par
- not required for the initial ER diagram render.\par
\par
\b 6. Backend Output You Should Depend On\b0\par
For your frontend work, the best contract is the /schema response only.\par
\par
Use these fields directly:\par
- tables[].name\par
- tables[].columns[]\par
- tables[].primary_key[]\par
- tables[].foreign_keys[]\par
- relationships[]\par
\par
Frontend meaning:\par
- one table = one node,\par
- one relationship = one edge,\par
- primary_key marks PK fields,\par
- foreign_keys mark FK fields and define edge labels,\par
- relationship type helps determine cardinality labels like N:1.\par
\par
\b 7. Frontend Files Most Relevant to You\b0\par
Folder: frontend/src/\par
\par
Important files:\par
- frontend/src/components/ERDiagram.jsx\par
- frontend/src/pages/SchemaPage.jsx\par
- frontend/src/data/db.js\par
\par
Current status:\par
- ERDiagram.jsx currently renders from hardcoded mock arrays ER_NODES and ER_LINKS.\par
- SchemaPage.jsx also uses hardcoded schema table data.\par
- frontend/src/data/db.js contains mock relationship and schema data.\par
\par
Meaning for your work:\par
- the UI structure already exists,\par
- but it is not yet connected to the real Schema Intelligence Engine,\par
- so your job is mainly to replace mock schema data with backend-driven data.\par
\par
\b 8. Recommended Frontend Flow for Your ER Diagram\b0\par
Safe step-by-step plan:\par
\par
Step 1:\par
- call GET /schema?infer=true from the frontend.\par
\par
Step 2:\par
- transform the response into graph data:\par
  node fields should include table name, columns, PKs, FKs, optional row count, and color.\par
  edge fields should include source, target, relationship type, FK column, and cardinality label.\par
\par
Step 3:\par
- pass nodes and edges into ERDiagram instead of importing ER_NODES and ER_LINKS from mock data.\par
\par
Step 4:\par
- keep rendering logic in the frontend only.\par
\par
Step 5:\par
- later add interactivity such as click-to-highlight, hover relationship details, and filtering.\par
\par
\b 9. Suggested Graph Shape for React/D3\b0\par
Recommended node shape:\par
\f1
{\pard
{\pntext\f1 1.\tab}{\par
  id: "orders",\par
  label: "orders",\par
  cols: 8,\par
  rows: null,\par
  pk: "order_id",\par
  fields: ["order_id (PK)", "customer_id (FK)", "status"],\par
  columns: [...],\par
  primaryKey: ["order_id"],\par
  foreignKeys: [...]\par
}\par
}
\f0
\par
Recommended edge shape:\par
\f1
{\pard
{\pntext\f1 1.\tab}{\par
  id: "orders-customers-0",\par
  source: "orders",\par
  target: "customers",\par
  type: "many-to-one",\par
  card: "N:1",\par
  via: "customer_id",\par
  fromColumn: "customer_id",\par
  toColumn: "customer_id"\par
}\par
}
\f0
\par
This shape fits the current D3 component well.\par
\par
\b 10. Recommended Boundaries to Avoid Team Conflicts\b0\par
Because multiple teammates are working in the same repo, keep your changes narrow.\par
\par
Preferred files to modify for your task:\par
- frontend/src/components/ERDiagram.jsx\par
- frontend/src/pages/SchemaPage.jsx or the page where the ER diagram lives\par
- a new frontend utility file for schema-to-graph mapping\par
- a new frontend API helper file if needed\par
\par
Avoid changing unless necessary:\par
- backend/main.py\par
- backend/routes/query.py\par
- teammate-owned service logic in backend/services/\par
- Schema Intelligence Engine internals unless there is a clear bug blocking frontend integration\par
\par
Best collaboration strategy:\par
- treat /schema as a stable API contract,\par
- avoid backend refactors,\par
- do not rename backend routes,\par
- do not change response shapes unless the team explicitly agrees,\par
- prefer additive frontend files over modifying many existing files at once.\par
\par
\b 11. Practical Working Strategy for You\b0\par
If you want to use Claude or Windsurf effectively, give them this narrow task framing:\par
\par
I am working only on the frontend custom ER diagram engine. The backend Schema Intelligence Engine already provides GET /schema, GET /profile, GET /preview, POST /upload, and POST /connect. I should mostly consume GET /schema and render a clean interactive ER diagram in React using D3. Please avoid backend refactors and keep changes limited to frontend data fetching, schema-to-graph conversion, layout, and interaction.\par
