\b 13. Minimal MVP for Your Part\b0\par
Your first safe deliverable should be:\par
- fetch /schema from the Schema Intelligence Engine,\par
- transform the response into nodes and edges,\par
- render the tables and FK relationships in React,\par
- show PK and FK markers,\par
- keep current styling as much as possible,\par
- avoid changing unrelated pages.\par
\par
\b 14. Nice Improvements After MVP\b0\par
Only after the basic flow works:\par
- better DAG or dagre layout,\par
- highlight connected tables on click,\par
- hover card with relationship info,\par
- collapse long column lists,\par
- filter/search tables,\par
- zoom-to-fit,\par
- performance tuning for 20 to 50 tables.\par
\par
\b 15. Final Advice\b0\par
For your part, think of the backend as a data provider and the frontend as the real implementation area.\par
The lowest-conflict path is: use GET /schema, keep backend untouched, and focus on a clean graph transformation plus a strong React/D3 rendering layer.\par
\par
Prepared for frontend ER diagram work and team-safe integration.\par
